  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 929 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nTonight and Friday...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this evening depicts a \nmid-level ridge centered over the Mid-South, with the local area \npositioned along the northern periphery of the ridge. This will \ncontinue to allow a series of shortwave disturbances to round the \nridge and bring periodic precipitation chances to the region. \nAnother round of thunderstorms is expected tonight as a shortwave \nimpulse moves from the Front Range towards the mid-MO Valley. \nIncreasing forcing for ascent, combined with a strengthening LLJ \nnosing into eastern Nebraska, should support the development of west-\nto-east oriented cluster of widely scattered thunderstorms along a \ntheta-e gradient around midnight. Meanwhile, thunderstorms have also \ndeveloped across northeast KS along a northeastward-advancing \nsurface low and associated front, and some of this activity may \nprogress into southeast NE and southwest IA overnight.\n\nA few strong to severe storms will be possible, with a corridor of \n1750-2500 J/kg of MLCAPE extending across eastern NE into western IA \nand decreasing northward. Deep-layer shear will be a limiting \nfactor, with only 20-25 kts of bulk shear in place. Even so, this \nmay be sufficient for a few stronger pulses capable of producing \nisolated pockets of hail (up to 1-1.25\") and damaging wind gusts (up \nto 60 mph). Attention will then turn toward the heavy-rainfall and \nflash-flooding potential, as training thunderstorms will encounter \nPWATs of 2-2.25 inches and warm-cloud depth of 4-4.5 km, supporting \nefficient rainfall production. CAM guidance continues to suggest \nlocalized totals of 2.5-3.5 inches within a relatively narrow east-\nto-west corridor, with the primary uncertainty remaining the \nlatitudinal placement of this band. Recent guidance has trended the \nheaviest rainfall corridor southward from northeast Nebraska toward \nareas along and just north of the Platte River Valley. This would \nshift the greatest rainfall amounts from some locations that \nreceived the heaviest totals Wednesday night. However, this narrow \naxis will need to be monitored near the saturated soils.\n\nStorm intensity and coverage will gradually decrease overnight, \nthought scattered showers may linger into Friday morning as an MCV \ndrifts in from the west. By Friday afternoon, a surface boundary \nwill lift northward into the area, returning southerly low-level \nflow and allowing temperatures and moisture to increase. Highs \nshould reach the low to mid 90s across southeast NE and southwest \nIA, while more persistent cloud cover and northerly low-level flow \nnorth of the front hold temperatures in the mid 70s across northeast \nNE.\n\nAdditional thunderstorm development will be possible Friday \nafternoon and evening across southeast NE and southwest IA as we \nremain in a WAA regime, the MCV meanders across the area, and modest \ninstability redevelops. Deep-layer shear will again be somewhat \nlimited at around 25 kts, though a strong to severe storm cannot be \nruled out. Initial development will likely be fairly isolated, with \ncoverage increasing into the evening as the LLJ strengthens. The \nbulk of this later activity should remain south of Interstate-80 \nwhere the better instability and area of convergence resides. \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nHigh temperatures Saturday will again range from the low 90s across \nsoutheast NE and southwest IA to the lower 80s towards northeast NE. \nAdditional precipitation chances are expected as the stalled boundary \nremains draped across the southern portion of the area. An initial \nround of showers and thunderstorms may move through during the \nmorning as a remnant MCV passes nearby, supporting 20-30% PoPs. \nChances increase again Saturday evening as another shortwave \ndisturbance approaches the region, with PoPs increasing to 50-75%. \nQuestions remain regarding the extent of lingering cloud cover and \nsubsequent destabilization, though another round of strong to severe \nstorms will be possible, primarily south of Interstate-80. \n\nCooler conditions are expected Sunday and Monday as the stalled \nboundary is finally pushed south of the area, allowing more \npersistent northerly flow and a brief period of CAA to settle in. \nHighs will generally range from the upper 70s to mid 80s. \nTemperatures through the remainder of the work week are expected to \nhang around the upper 70s to upper 80s. Periodic precipitation \nchances around 15-30% will continue as disturbances traverse the \narea, with improved chances (30-60%) Tuesday evening.",
